From: Rene Vogelaar <revogelaar@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] tests: honor a caller-supplied GoogleTest filter

tests/main.cpp assigned its built-in negative filter AFTER InitGoogleTest()
had already parsed and stored --gtest_filter, so the assignment overwrote
whatever the caller asked for. GTEST_FILTER was discarded for the same
reason. The binary therefore had no supported way to select a subset: every
invocation ran all ~2900 registered tests, and a run aborted inside an
unrelated pre-existing failure long before reaching the suite under test.

Testing GTEST_FLAG(filter) after InitGoogleTest() cannot fix this, because
InitGoogleTest consumes the option out of argv -- afterwards "the caller
asked for *" and "the caller asked for nothing" are indistinguishable.

Decide before InitGoogleTest() instead, by inspecting argv and the
environment directly, and install the built-in exclusion list only when the
caller supplied neither form.

Verified both branches on the built binary:

    --gtest_filter=EmSteeringSerializer.*   -> banner prints, 7 tests run
    GTEST_FILTER=EmSteeringSerializer.*     -> banner prints, 7 tests run
    no caller filter                        -> 2268 of 2917 tests listed,
                                               i.e. exclusions still applied

Upstream develop (43f7f7127c8035ff18aa7a78eb82c1b22795ef1a) still carries
the unconditional override, so this is suitable to send upstream as-is.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
diff --git a/tests/main.cpp b/tests/main.cpp
index ece2896e..fa55e5ac 100644
--- a/tests/main.cpp
+++ b/tests/main.cpp
@@ -17,10 +17,34 @@
  * limitations under the License.
  */
 #include <gtest/gtest.h>
+#include <string.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
 
 int main(int argc, char **argv) {
+    // Decide BEFORE InitGoogleTest whether the caller asked for a specific
+    // filter: InitGoogleTest consumes --gtest_filter out of argv, so testing
+    // GTEST_FLAG(filter) afterwards cannot distinguish "caller asked for *"
+    // from "caller asked for nothing". Assigning the built-in exclusion list
+    // unconditionally after InitGoogleTest -- as this file used to -- overrides
+    // both --gtest_filter and GTEST_FILTER, so no subset can ever be selected
+    // and every invocation runs the entire binary.
+    bool caller_supplied_filter = (getenv("GTEST_FILTER") != NULL);
+    for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++) {
+        if (argv[i] != NULL && strncmp(argv[i], "--gtest_filter=", 15) == 0) {
+            caller_supplied_filter = true;
+            break;
+        }
+    }
+
     ::testing::InitGoogleTest(&argc, argv);
 
+    if (!caller_supplied_filter)
+
+    // Only apply the built-in exclusion list when the caller did not ask for a
+    // specific filter. Assigning unconditionally here overrides both
+    // --gtest_filter and GTEST_FILTER, which makes it impossible to run a single
+    // test or suite -- every invocation runs the whole binary.
+    if (::testing::GTEST_FLAG(filter) == "*") {
 ::testing::GTEST_FLAG(filter) =
     "-dm_ap_mld_t_Test.DecodeValidJsonObjectWithNullParentID:"
     "dm_ap_mld_t_Test.DecodeNullJsonObjectWithValidParentID:"
@@ -714,5 +738,6 @@ int main(int argc, char **argv) {
     "em_ctrl_t_Test.input_listen_default:"
     "em_ctrl_t_Test.delete_nodes_valid:"
     "em_ctrl_t_Test.delete_node_existing_ieee80211n_24";
+    }
     return RUN_ALL_TESTS();
 }
